.TH XCLIPY 1 2026-11-04 Xclipy "Xclipy manual"
.SH NAME
xclipy \- clipboard history and emoji picker
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBxclipy\fP [\fB\-\-background\fP | \fB\-\-show\fP | \fB\-\-emoji\fP | \fB\-\-settings\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
\fBxclipy\fP keeps what you copy and opens it in an overlay at the mouse
pointer, or in the bottom right corner of the screen when there is none. Choosing
an item pastes it into the application you were typing in. An emoji picker
opens the same way, once turned on in the settings.
.LP
It runs from login without a window, and its keyboard shortcuts (Super+V, and
Super+. for the emojis) open the overlays. They are the desktop's own custom shortcuts in
Cinnamon, MATE and Xfce, added the first time Xclipy runs there.
.LP
Without an option, \fBxclipy\fP opens the overlay, like \fB\-\-show\fP.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-background
Start without opening the overlay (as at login).
.TP
.B \-\-show
Open the clipboard history, or close it if it is open.
.TP
.B \-\-emoji
Open the emojis, or close them if they are open. Nothing happens while the
emojis are turned off in the settings.
.TP
.B \-\-settings
Open the settings. In Cinnamon, they are also in System Settings, Clipboard.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/xclipy/history.json
The history, pinned items included. Passwords are not in it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/xclipy/images/
The copied images, when the settings keep them.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBcinnamon-settings\fP(1)
